    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i have a physics exam coming up and i'm struggling a bit with the concept of quantum entanglement. can you give me a concise, non-mathematical summary of what it actually is and how it's different from just regular classical correlation? thanks",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Quantum entanglement is a phenomenon where two particles become connected in such a way that their properties, like spin or momentum, are correlated, even when separated by large distances. It's like a secret handshake between the particles, where they're \"in sync\" even if they're on opposite sides of the universe. \n\nImagine two dancers performing a choreographed routine, where their movements are perfectly synchronized, even if they're on opposite sides of the stage. That's roughly what's happening with entangled particles - their properties are \"dancing\" together, even if they're separated by vast distances. \n\nNow, classical correlation is like two dancers who happen to be in the same position at the same time, but they're not necessarily \"in sync\" - they're just coincidentally aligned. Entanglement is different because it's not just a coincidence, but a fundamental property of the particles themselves. \n\nThink of it like a pair of twins who are connected by an invisible thread, where their thoughts and actions are somehow linked, even if they're on opposite sides of the world. That's roughly the idea behind entanglement - two particles are connected in a way that transcends space and time.",
